ga River in central Russia. This state, however, divided into many distinct and competing, even warring principalities after the death of Yaroslav. By the middle of the 12th century, the influence of Kievan Rus\' had waned definitively."<br>"The historical province (principality of Novgorod consisted of an area with a radius of roughly 200-250 km" surrounded by five other areas.<br>Middle 10th century "Novgorod consisted of three small settlements, possibly connected with three different ethnic entities." So unimportant as not to be mentioned in lists of Russian towns in Russo-Byzantine treaties.'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2558, 'temperature': 0.2})
